Here is my analysis of what needs editing, what is redundant, and how the design could be more efficient.

1. High-Level Redundancy: The "Two Settings" Problem
You currently have two Settings.jsx files:

src/pages/Settings.jsx
src/Settings.jsx
The Issue: The src/Settings.jsx version is significantly more advanced (it has seeding logic, separate popup modals for adding/deleting, and business profile settings). However, src/pages/Settings.jsx has the machine revenue logic. The Fix: You should merge these into a single file. Having two files doing nearly the same thing is a recipe for "ghost bugs" where you fix a price in one place but it doesn't show up in the other.

2. UI & Styling Inconsistencies
Duplicate Icons: The Icon component and the D path constants are redefined in almost every file (Modals.jsx, both Settings.jsx files, etc.).
Style Bloat: Styles like inputStyle, labelStyle, and pillBtnStyle are copy-pasted across files. If you decide to change the "primary" color or the "border-radius" of your inputs, you currently have to edit 4 or 5 different files.
Efficient Way: Create a src/components/ui folder.
Move the Icon system to Icon.jsx.
Move standard inputs to a SharedStyles.js or use a CSS Module.
This ensures that an input in "Settings" looks identical to an input in "New Invoice" without re-writing the code.
3. Modal Logic: "Review vs. Add" Buttons
Your checkpoint mentions: "the review and add thing is still there..i need them to be separate buttons".

Current Design: You are using a "Wizard" flow (Step 1: Form -> Step 2: Preview). Why it's clunky: The user has to click "Preview" just to get to the "Add" button. If they are confident, this is an extra click. Better Design:

Instead of a two-step state, keep the form visible at all times.
Place three buttons in the footer: [ Cancel ] [ Preview (Opens in New Tab/Popup) ] [ Create Invoice ].
Or, use a "Split View" modal (Desktop only) where the form is on the left and a live-updating preview is on the right. This removes the need for a "Preview" step entirely.
4. Data Handling Concerns
Hardcoded Mocks in "Centralized" Modals: RecordPaymentModal in Modals.jsx still uses mockRecords. Since your Receivables and Payables pages are already fetching backend data, this modal should accept availableRecords as a prop or fetch them itself. It's currently a "live" app with a "static" heart.
Validation Logic: Your validation is currently very manual (e.g., if (!form.client) ...). As your forms get more complex (like the Invoice line items), this will become hard to manage.
Inconsistency in NewProposalModal: You have a form.total field (manual input) AND a totalValue (calculated). This is dangerous. If the user types "10,000" but the items add up to "12,000", which one is "truth"? You should remove the manual total input and only use the calculated value.
5. Specific File Critiques
Modals.jsx
Redundant total logic: Both NewInvoiceModal and NewProposalModal calculate totals. You could create a utility function calculateTotal(items) to handle this once.
ModalWrapper Scroll: Your body has maxHeight: '65vh'. For long invoices with many items, this might be too small on some screens.
PreviewModal.jsx
Redundant: Now that your individual modals (NewInvoiceModal, etc.) have their own built-in previews, this generic PreviewModal is becoming redundant. It only shows raw JSON/text, which isn't useful for a "Business Document" preview. It should be replaced by a PrintTemplate component that formats the data as a real invoice.
api/client.js (referenced in migrations)
Caching: Your migration guide says API responses are cached for 30 seconds. While efficient, ensure that after a POST (like creating a new invoice), you have a way to "bust" that cache so the new invoice shows up immediately in the list.
