A Shared Component Library: Move Icon, Button, Input, and Badge into their own files.
PDF/Print Engine: Since only Invoices can make PDFs currently, you need a shared DocumentGenerator that can handle Proposals and Jobs too.
Real Data Integration: Wire the RecordPaymentModal to the actual backend search.
Z-Index Audit: You have zIndex: 80 in some places and zIndex: 1000 in others. Standardize your "layers" (Backdrop, Modal, Tooltip, Toast).
1. Architectural Redundancy: The "Two Settings" Conflict
The biggest structural issue is the existence of two separate Settings.jsx files (src/Settings.jsx and src/pages/Settings.jsx).

The Problem: src/Settings.jsx contains the advanced logic for seeding machines and business profiles, while src/pages/Settings.jsx focuses on machine revenue logic.
Why it's bad: This creates "split truth." If you update a pricing item or machine capability in one file, it won't reflect in the other. It also makes debugging difficult because you never know which component is actually being rendered by the router.
The Fix: These should be consolidated into one master Settings page that handles both configuration (seeding/pricing) and reporting (revenue).
2. UI & UX Workflow: The "Wizard" vs. "Action Bar"
Currently, your modals (like NewInvoiceModal) use a two-step state logic ('form' or 'preview').

The Problem: This forces a "Wizard" flow. A user must click "Preview" to reach the "Confirm" button.
The Inefficiency: For power users who fill out forms quickly, this is an unnecessary extra click.
The Better Way: As noted in your checkpoint, the form should remain visible at all times. The footer should feature three distinct, high-level actions: [ Cancel ], [ Preview PDF ] (which could open a new tab or a non-blocking popup), and [ Create Invoice ]. This removes the "Back to Edit" cycle and streamlines the workflow.
3. Styling & Component Inconsistency
You have significant "Style Bloat" across almost every file.

The Problem: Standard objects like inputStyle, labelStyle, and pillBtnStyle are hardcoded at the top of Modals.jsx, Settings.jsx, and various page files.
The Risk: If you decide to change your "Primary" blue to a different shade, you currently have to hunt down and manually change it in 5+ different files.
The Efficiency Gap: The Icon component and the D path constants are also redefined everywhere.
The Proper Way: You need a src/components/ui folder. Moving Icon.jsx, Button.jsx, and a SharedStyles.js (or CSS modules) there ensures that an input on the Settings page looks and behaves exactly like an input in a New Invoice modal without duplicating code.
4. Data Handling & Logic "Ghost Bugs"
There are a few places where the logic is internally inconsistent:

NewProposalModal: This modal has a form.total (manual input) AND a totalValue (calculated from items). This is dangerous. If the user types "10,000" but the items add up to "12,000," the system doesn't know which is correct.
Hardcoded Mocks: Modals like RecordPaymentModal still rely on internal mockRecords. This creates a "static heart" inside a live app. These modals should accept data as props or fetch from the same backend endpoints your main pages use.
5. Responsiveness & Mobile View
You mentioned that minimizing the window causes things to break, especially the top bar.

Top Bar & Search: The top bar layout likely fails because the search bar or its container has a hardcoded width (e.g., maxWidth: '400px' or width: '90%') that doesn't play well with the sidebar toggle or other header elements.
Flex Behavior: Without a global CSS reset or a consistent flex-wrap strategy, items that look good on a 1920x1080 screen will overlap or disappear on mobile.
6. Functionality Gaps
Your observation about the "Reports" and other buttons not working is correct. The development focus has been heavily weighted toward Invoices and Proposals.

Inconsistency: Invoices is the "Gold Standard" in your project—it has PDF logic, line-item builders, and backend sync.
The Goal: To achieve coherency, the same "CRUD" (Create, Read, Update, Delete) patterns used in Invoices need to be replicated in Expenses, Vendors, and Reports so that no button in the UI leads to an alert("Coming soon") or a dead link.
